    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with an aggressive stance, anchored by **Vinnie Pasquantino** (76.21) and **Corbin Carroll** (73.35). **Vinnie Pasquantino** is our strongest play, benefiting from a soft matchup against Arrighetti and a massive +18% SP Skill boost, while **Corbin Carroll** offers elite speed upside against a vulnerable Lambert. **Mickey Moniak** (73.14) also climbs into the must-start tier; his 14% boost from dynamic platoon advantages against Leahy makes him a primary engine for our 5x5 Roto chase. Similarly, **James Wood** (67.15) remains a high-floor play at the top of the order, where his elite 0.417 xwOBA and favorable base speed dynamics against Wrobleski demand inclusion despite the \"Lineup Pending\" status.\n\nOur most difficult bench decision involved **Josh Naylor** (55.62), who sits just outside our active efficiency threshold. While **Josh Naylor** provides reliable production, he was edged out by the superior matchup context for **Isaac Paredes** (58.54) and **Austin Riley** (57.08). While **Austin Riley** is relegated to the bottom of the order, his specific historical BvP dominance\u2014a +8% boost over 63 plate appearances\u2014narrowly kept him ahead of the field. Meanwhile, **Max Muncy** (38.58) fell well below our 40-point efficiency floor due to a brutal matchup against Woo and historical struggles, ensuring he remains on the bench to preserve our valuable game caps. \n\nRisk management for today centers on **James Wood**, whose projected score relies on the assumption of a leadoff role. Should that shift, his efficiency metrics may recalibrate. We are also monitoring **Trevor Story** (70.35); while his raw score is high thanks to a 16% park factor boost, he is facing an \"Elite Hold\" pitcher who could suppress his stolen base potential. We are proceeding with this configuration to maximize our SB scarcity advantage while maintaining defensive coverage against the league-average xERA starters on the opposing mound.",
